<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § P4: an inspector authored as markup, with no code-behind at all.

    There is no `@code` block and there is no C# in this file. Every row is the row the generated
    inspector would have drawn — `PropertyField` resolves the member against the ambient edit target
    and hands it to whatever drawer claims it, so the reset button, the prefab override bar, the
    multi-select mixed state and the undo all arrive without being asked for.

    ⚠ What this buys over the flat list it replaces is *order and grouping*. The generated inspector
    draws members in declaration order because that is the only order it has; a brush has three
    unrelated things on it — what shape it is, how a stroke is laid down, and what pattern masks it —
    and reading them as one column of eleven sliders is what makes people stop looking for the one
    they want. `[Header]` on the type could say some of this, but not that Spacing belongs with
    Rotation rather than with Falloff.

    ⚠ And the groups are `Expander`s because that is what the generated inspector's own headings are,
    so this reads as the same panel rearranged rather than as a second kind of inspector.

    ⚠ Shape, Rotation, Angle and the Pattern group are not here, and that is #1098 rather than an
    oversight. Every one of them acts only through an `IBrushMask`, and no terrain or foliage tool
    supplies one — `TerrainBrush.WeightAt` paints the plain disc for a null mask — so the four rows
    were controls an artist could move and watch nothing happen. They come back when a mask source
    does; `BrushInspectorMarkupTests.Every_row_the_brush_inspector_offers_changes_the_ground_a_stroke_writes`
    is what refuses a row that moves no ground.
-->
@component TerrainBrushInspector
@namespace Vixen.Editor.Terrain
@tag terrain-brush-inspector
@using Vixen.Editor.Inspector
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls

<Expander Label="Shape" IsExpanded="true">
    <PropertyField Path="Radius" />
    <PropertyField Path="Falloff" />
    <PropertyField Path="Curve" />
</Expander>

<Expander Label="Stroke" IsExpanded="true">
    <PropertyField Path="Strength" />
    <PropertyField Path="Spacing" />
</Expander>
